  <section class="wrap section standalone-case"><h2>Whose waiting time improves?</h2><p>A lower average can hide a long wait for an individual job. I explored that trade-off using a fictional workshop with one test bench, known job durations and a two-minute changeover.</p><p>Both policies choose only from released jobs. FCFS selects the earliest arrival; SPT selects the shortest ready job. Neither sees future arrivals or claims to produce a globally optimal schedule.</p><figure><img src="/media/waiting-time-comparison.png" alt="Two scatterplots compare average and longest waits under FCFS and SPT across 22 synthetic scenarios. The steady-arrival case has a longer maximum wait under SPT." loading="lazy" width="1920" height="800"><figcaption>Actual experiment output. Below the diagonal means less waiting under SPT; above it means more. The orange diamond preserves the steady-arrival trade-off.</figcaption></figure>
